window.VTMB=window.VTMB||{};window.VTMB.dgrAdv=[{"id":"2b70VAegIg10GcQJ","n":"Blood Curse Immunity (Ghoul)","ed":"dgr","kind":"merit","cat":"Ghoul and Revenant","tag":"Merit (•••••)","dots":[5],"sum":"You have discovered that you are resistant to the Clan or bloodline flaws present in the blood you drink.","html":"<p><em>Merit (•••••)</em></p>\n<p>You have discovered that you are resistant to the Clan or bloodline flaws present in the blood you drink. Your domitor might be a lower Generation Nosferatu, who lives in the sewers because their appearance is so hideous, and yet your appearance will not change. Should members of the Tremere or Tzimisce Clans learn of your Blood Flaw Immunity, you may be sought after for further study. After all, if a ghoul’s biology is such that you do not suffer as much as your domitor, surely there’s a “cure” for the flaws present in every Clan’s or bloodline’s blood, not to mention the potnetial for curing revenant Family Afflictions.</p>\n<p>This Merit no longer applies if your ghoul or revenant is Embraced and is refunded.</p>","src":{"book":"Dark Gifts: Disciplines Restored","page":"386"}},{"id":"320ZxBZUGQeVnafL","n":"Nuit Mode","ed":"dgr","kind":"merit","cat":"Physical","tag":"Merit (••)","dots":[2],"sum":"The Cainite’s body does not revert to it’s death-state each night, enabling them to keep new haircuts and body modifications. They can mend these changes anytime as if they were Aggravated damage.","html":"<p><em>Merit (••)</em></p>\n<p>The Cainite’s body does not revert to it’s death-state each night, enabling them to keep new haircuts and body modifications. They can mend these changes anytime as if they were Aggravated damage. This does not work for characters with BP higher than 1</p>","src":{"book":"Dark Gifts: Disciplines Restored","page":"394"}},{"id":"3iEVgtfqiYC3DCCL","n":"Faded (Obfuscate)","ed":"dgr","kind":"flaw","cat":"Ingrained Discipline","tag":"Flaw (•)","dots":[1],"sum":"The vampire spends so much time hiding from others that they have begun to forget who they truly are. This lack of conviction weakens their moral fortitude and makes them second-guess decisions.","html":"<p><em>Flaw (•)</em></p>\n<p>The vampire spends so much time hiding from others that they have begun to forget who they truly are. This lack of conviction weakens their moral fortitude and makes them second-guess decisions. When making Degeneration tests, the vampire rolls one fewer die (this cannot bring the pool to 0).</p>","src":{"book":"Dark Gifts: Disciplines Restored","page":"389"}},{"id":"3vN5uxKwK2fTHALN","n":"Eidetic Memory","ed":"dgr","kind":"merit","cat":"Mental","tag":"Merit (••)","dots":[2],"sum":"You remember what you see and hear with perfect detail. Documents, events and conversations, can be committed to memory with only minor concentration.","html":"<p><em>Merit (••)</em></p>\n<p>You remember what you see and hear with perfect detail. Documents, events and conversations, can be committed to memory with only minor concentration. Under stressful conditions involving numerous distractions, you must make a Resolve + Awareness roll (Difficulty 2) to summon enough concentration to absorb what your senses detect.</p>","src":{"book":"Dark Gifts: Disciplines Restored","page":"392"}},{"id":"5ASFgUz04XAyjjMQ","n":"Permanent Wound","ed":"dgr","kind":"flaw","cat":"Physical","tag":"Flaw (•••)","dots":[3],"sum":"You suffered injuries during your Embrace yor transformation somehow failed to repair or sustained heavy damage as a mortal. You have one point of Health less.","html":"<p><em>Flaw (•••)</em></p>\n<p>You suffered injuries during your Embrace yor transformation somehow failed to repair or sustained heavy damage as a mortal. You have one point of Health less.</p>\n<p>A five-dot version of this inflicts a dice penalty of -2 dice to all physical rolls adds +1 to all additional damage suffered.</p>","src":{"book":"Dark Gifts: Disciplines Restored","page":"395"}},{"id":"5LFhEFubrM2cdFVU","n":"Mawla","ed":"dgr","kind":"background","cat":"Backgrounds","tag":"","dots":[],"sum":"Mawla (meaning protector, friend or a master in Arabic) is now a category of Backgrounds to best show the specific types of relationships that can happen. If it is too much of a bother, you do not have to build your Mawla’s sheet.","html":"<p>Mawla (meaning protector, friend or a master in Arabic) is now a category of Backgrounds to best show the specific types of relationships that can happen. If it is too much of a bother, you do not have to build your Mawla’s sheet. Simply write their purpose such as teaching sorcery.</p>\n<p>You may not directly control your Mawla or benefit from their Advantages. It should happen rarely in situations created by the storyteller, usually at a price. They may provide teamwork on projects not related to Backgrounds with a trivial boon required for others (one trivial boon should buy at least 4 project rolls’ worth of teamwork). Unusual Mawlae may require more unusual payments, however.</p>\n<h3>Demon Master (Non-Ghost Only)</h3>\n<p>You have sworn fealty to a demon, trading Faith and other services for whatever you have managed to bargain with them. You may be a thrall to only a single demon at a time. The Demon Master has a maximum number of 5 dots, spread across power and benevolence showing the balance between what they their services.</p>\n<p>For Demon and Thrall mechanics around Arcana, pacts, Divinity and so on, see Appendix B, p. XX.</p>\n<p>This Background does not represent enslaved demons as they will never be permanently enslaved and will come back for revenge if they wish so. A thrall may not use any powers to enslave their master, but may use powers to summon them as normal.</p>\n<p>To demons, faith is a currency, fuel for their powers and even their existence. As a thrall, you will be required to do daily prayers or “religious” rituals or suffer a punishment from your master who can brutally sap faith from you at will, potentially killing you. A character may refuse to do this, but they will either be visited by a demon’s agent (or, worse, the demon themselves) or have their Faith ravaged from them (p. XX). A player character may be ravaged at the storyteller’s discretion for Bestial Failures, Messy Criticals or for story purposes unless the player invests in Benevolence.</p>\n<p>A thrall with this Background is provided with a Celestial Name of their master. If a Celestial Name is spoken aloud or silently in one’s mind, a person may attract attention from a demon who will be able to sense where they are and perceive the world through the senses of the one who attracted their attention. For this reason, uttering names such as “Kupala” and “Belial” as a joke within the presence of demonologists is considered a grave faux pas.</p>\n<p>This Background can only be obtained at character creation. Getting a demonic master during play should be free and fully tailored by the storyteller. Note that the chances of stumbling upon a “benevolent”, let alone “loving” demon master should be astronomically low. Demons of this Background are among the weaker ones. You will not get Belial or Asmodeus unless the Storyteller assigns him to you.</p>\n<p>Demons are always controlled by the storyteller, but the control may be ceded to a player for a combat scene to reduce the storyteller’s burden. Players do not build their own demons, but the Storyteller does in secret (after discussing the limits of the power with the player and its capabilities) or simply uses the Storyteller fiat to represent the demon.</p>\n<p>■ <strong>Power:</strong> </p>\n<p>• Petty Demon: Build them as an Average Individual. Divinity 2.</p>\n<p>•• Weak Demon: Build them as a Gifted Individual. Divinity 4</p>\n<p>••• Average Demon: Build them as a Gifted Individual. Divinity 6</p>\n<p>•••• Strong Demon: Build them as a Deadly Individual. Divinity 8.</p>\n<p>■ <strong>Benevolence:</strong> </p>\n<p>Without dots in Benevolence, the demon expects its full tax in Faith.</p>\n<p>•• Caring: The demon is…caring enough (as much as that word can mean in this context). They have fully exempted you from the “faith tax” either because they see potential and some other form of value in them or because you have managed to negotiate it in the pact. You will never be ravaged.</p>\n<p>••• Loving: Perhaps they have some genuine affection for your or are just pretending in order for their agenda to be advanced, they fully exempt you from the “faith tax” and may even aid you in life. Whenever you wish for them to show up, they will come in around 10 turns. The demon will speed up its journey if necessary to defend their investment, arriving in 3 turns if they sense their thrall is in danger and has been telepathically called for them.</p>","src":{"book":"Dark Gifts: Disciplines Restored","page":"381"}},{"id":"6cd4abzxgRwmUK5k","n":"Pale Aura","ed":"dgr","kind":"merit","cat":"Ghoul and Revenant","tag":"Merit (••)","dots":[2],"sum":"Due to some quirk of fate or your reaction to the Blood, your aura is naturally pale. Any colour your character’s aura takes has a pale cast to it, making you indistinguishable from a vampire when viewed by powers such as Scry the Soul.","html":"<p><em>Merit (••)</em></p>\n<p>Due to some quirk of fate or your reaction to the Blood, your aura is naturally pale. Any colour your character’s aura takes has a pale cast to it, making you indistinguishable from a vampire when viewed by powers such as Scry the Soul.</p>\n<p>This Merit can be of great use if you wish to impersonate a vampire for a time, but can also cause a dangerous misunderstanding or lead to your capture for further scrutiny.</p>\n<p>If a character viewing your aura scores 5 or more margins, they may notice your real aura behind the layer of pale.</p>","src":{"book":"Dark Gifts: Disciplines Restored","page":"386"}},{"id":"6lZB6OUmW29N0vyU","n":"Low Pain Threshold","ed":"dgr","kind":"flaw","cat":"Physical","tag":"Flaw (••)","dots":[2],"sum":"You are unable to deal with a lot of physical pain. When a character’s Health tracker is filled with damage equal to their Stamina, they become Impaired.","html":"<p><em>Flaw (••)</em></p>\n<p>You are unable to deal with a lot of physical pain. When a character’s Health tracker is filled with damage equal to their Stamina, they become Impaired.</p>","src":{"book":"Dark Gifts: Disciplines Restored","page":"395"}},{"id":"7VKxsdLg4eJKfCeZ","n":"Deaf","ed":"dgr","kind":"flaw","cat":"Physical","tag":"Flaw (•••)","dots":[3],"sum":"You cannot hear. While you may ignore some applications of Dominate, you may not hear warnings, and you have great difficulty in normal communication.","html":"<p><em>Flaw (•••)</em></p>\n<p>You cannot hear. While you may ignore some applications of Dominate, you may not hear warnings, and you have great difficulty in normal communication. Lose three dice on rolls that involve hearing, such as most perception rolls like spotting a surprise attack.</p>","src":{"book":"Dark Gifts: Disciplines Restored","page":"394"}},{"id":"7iAHHyWMkdQ7mXir","n":"Antitoxin Body","ed":"dgr","kind":"merit","cat":"Physical","tag":"Merit (•)","dots":[1],"sum":"Whether by drinking them in small doses to build immunity or by virtue of your blood, you are immune to mortal poisons, toxins, and drugs. This cannot be combined with other Substance Use Merits or Flaws.","html":"<p><em>Merit (•)</em></p>\n<p>Whether by drinking them in small doses to build immunity or by virtue of your blood, you are immune to mortal poisons, toxins, and drugs. This cannot be combined with other Substance Use Merits or Flaws.</p>","src":{"book":"Dark Gifts: Disciplines Restored","page":"394"}},{"id":"8sPHOOIFw2kwggRJ","n":"Untamed (Animalism)","ed":"dgr","kind":"flaw","cat":"Ingrained Discipline","tag":"Flaw (•)","dots":[1],"sum":"Such a tight relationship with the Beast comes at a cost, as it refuses to be leashed. Failing to Ride the Wave during Frenzy inflicts two points of Aggravated Willpower damage.","html":"<p><em>Flaw (•)</em></p>\n<p>Such a tight relationship with the Beast comes at a cost, as it refuses to be leashed. Failing to Ride the Wave during Frenzy inflicts two points of Aggravated Willpower damage.</p>","src":{"book":"Dark Gifts: Disciplines Restored","page":"388"}},{"id":"9Zj8X3X08g15L8I9","n":"Daymares (Auspex)","ed":"dgr","kind":"flaw","cat":"Ingrained Discipline","tag":"Flaw (•)","dots":[1],"sum":"While rarely recalling more than an acute sense of terror, the vampire’s daylight rest becomes plagued by horrifying visions, causing them to sweat blood. When awakening for the night, make two Rouse Checks instead of the usual one.","html":"<p><em>Flaw (•)</em></p>\n<p>While rarely recalling more than an acute sense of terror, the vampire’s daylight rest becomes plagued by horrifying visions, causing them to sweat blood. When awakening for the night, make two Rouse Checks instead of the usual one.</p>","src":{"book":"Dark Gifts: Disciplines Restored","page":"388"}},{"id":"9dJ0dqzMoRKs5XSi","n":"Deranged","ed":"dgr","kind":"flaw","cat":"Mental","tag":"Flaw (•)","dots":[1],"sum":"Either a pre-existing condition, a curse or the trauma of damnation has sown madness in you. For each dot in of this Flaw, a character has two triggers tied to a Derangement, to a maximum of 5 flaws or 10 triggers.","html":"<p><em>Flaw (•)</em></p>\n<p>Either a pre-existing condition, a curse or the trauma of damnation has sown madness in you. For each dot in of this Flaw, a character has two triggers tied to a Derangement, to a maximum of 5 flaws or 10 triggers. A person may have multiple separate Deranged Flaws, each tied to a separate Derangement. See p. XX for Derangement system and triggers.</p>\n<p>■ <strong>Faunt of Heart:</strong> You are easily sickened</p>\n<p>•• by the sight of blood. Whenever you witness a gory scene or blood spilled, you must roll Willpower (Dfficulty 2). If the roll fails, you are overcome with nausea for the rest of the scene. While this doesn’t mean you spend the time vomiting (unless you roll a Total Failure; vomiting vampires raise their Hunger by one) it does mean that all non-tracker pools sufer a die penalty.</p>","src":{"book":"Dark Gifts: Disciplines Restored","page":"392"}},{"id":"AlUuUXpHtGiON4Qm","n":"Monsterous Skin (Serpentis)","ed":"dgr","kind":"flaw","cat":"Ingrained Discipline","tag":"Flaw (•)","dots":[1],"sum":"The vampire’s usage of Serpentis brings them closer to their god…but not in a way one might expect.","html":"<p><em>Flaw (•)</em></p>\n<p>The vampire’s usage of Serpentis brings them closer to their god…but not in a way one might expect. Each night, they awaken with a condition that can be described as their own skin that has taken on properties of scaly snakes, feathery monsters from folk tales or other mythical beasts. To shed this skin, the user must scrub it off, suffering two points of Superficial Health damage.</p>","src":{"book":"Dark Gifts: Disciplines Restored","page":"390"}},{"id":"ApJkJouBo1IZtMaC","n":"Blunt (Dominate)","ed":"dgr","kind":"flaw","cat":"Ingrained Discipline","tag":"Flaw (•)","dots":[1],"sum":"The strain of enforcing their will upon others erodes something critical in the mind of the dominator, making mundane social interactions fraught. Vampires with this Flaw cannot spend Willpower to reroll a Social test.","html":"<p><em>Flaw (•)</em></p>\n<p>The strain of enforcing their will upon others erodes something critical in the mind of the dominator, making mundane social interactions fraught. Vampires with this Flaw cannot spend Willpower to reroll a Social test.</p>","src":{"book":"Dark Gifts: Disciplines Restored","page":"389"}},{"id":"BjKqVJOrq1iaup4y","n":"Phobia","ed":"dgr","kind":"flaw","cat":"Mental","tag":"Flaw (•)","dots":[1],"sum":"You have an extreme amount of fear towards a specific stimulus, defined when you take this Flaw. When you encounter this stimulus, you must make a Difficulty 2 Fear Frenzy test. Must be something not-incredibly-rare.","html":"<p><em>Flaw (•)</em></p>\n<p>You have an extreme amount of fear towards a specific stimulus, defined when you take this Flaw. When you encounter this stimulus, you must make a Difficulty 2 Fear Frenzy test. Must be something not-incredibly-rare.</p>","src":{"book":"Dark Gifts: Disciplines Restored","page":"392"}},{"id":"CBRV7msvIDrSnmYx","n":"Medium","ed":"dgr","kind":"merit","cat":"Numen","tag":"Merit (••)","dots":[2],"sum":"You possess the natural affinity to sense and hear spirits, ghosts, and shades. Though you cannot see them, you can sense them, speak to them, hear them and, through pleading or cajoling, draw them to your presence.","html":"<p><em>Merit (••)</em></p>\n<p>You possess the natural affinity to sense and hear spirits, ghosts, and shades. Though you cannot see them, you can sense them, speak to them, hear them and, through pleading or cajoling, draw them to your presence. You may call upon them for aid or advice, but there will always be a price.</p>","src":{"book":"Dark Gifts: Disciplines Restored","page":"393"}},{"id":"ChmEV0Ne0k62eIVW","n":"Dead Soul (Thrall Only)","ed":"dgr","kind":"flaw","cat":"Infernal","tag":"Flaw (••••)","dots":[4],"sum":"For whatever reason, you may not passively regenerate Faith like a common human and instead must find other ways to power your infernal gifts. Vampires have this by default.","html":"<p><em>Flaw (••••)</em></p>\n<p>For whatever reason, you may not passively regenerate Faith like a common human and instead must find other ways to power your infernal gifts. Vampires have this by default.</p>","src":{"book":"Dark Gifts: Disciplines Restored","page":"387"}},{"id":"Ea2a1mprgkiXDnX0","n":"Devoted","ed":"dgr","kind":"merit","cat":"Mental","tag":"Merit (••–••••)","dots":[2,3,4],"sum":"You are devoted to service to or reverence of a higher power, be it Christ, Allah, Odin, or some other deity. Once per game session, you may pray for one full turn to your divine patron for aid and guidance.","html":"<p><em>Merit (••–••••)</em></p>\n<p>You are devoted to service to or reverence of a higher power, be it Christ, Allah, Odin, or some other deity. Once per game session, you may pray for one full turn to your divine patron for aid and guidance. In game terms, your character receives one to two points of temporary Willpower (half the value of the Merit) for the scene. If these points are spent in a way that is frivolous or antithetical to the character’s Ethics, this Merit may be rescinded until they can make amends. These points of Willpower are not part of the tracker, it merely allows you to reroll up to two pools or spend them to activate a power...</p>","src":{"book":"Dark Gifts: Disciplines Restored","page":"392"}},{"id":"FUp4EMrcJjHtEBLp","n":"Unnerving Shadow (Obtenebration)","ed":"dgr","kind":"flaw","cat":"Ingrained Discipline","tag":"Flaw (•)","dots":[1],"sum":"Your shadow appears almost sentient, moving at its own accord.","html":"<p><em>Flaw (•)</em></p>\n<p>Your shadow appears almost sentient, moving at its own accord. While it will never really try to endanger you by being a masquerade breach in of itself, it still does hinder your ability to interact with mortals in a normal manner as it unnerves others. Gain a three-dice penalty to interact with normal mortals in a non-threatening manner unless you are in an area where your shadow will not be visible.</p>","src":{"book":"Dark Gifts: Disciplines Restored","page":"389"}},{"id":"FVlYGVvrpa7V1uZ5","n":"Supernatural Awareness","ed":"dgr","kind":"merit","cat":"Numen","tag":"Merit (•••)","dots":[3],"sum":"You can sense the hidden patterns of the world, seeing through illusions and magics where others would blindly believe.","html":"<p><em>Merit (•••)</em></p>\n<p>You can sense the hidden patterns of the world, seeing through illusions and magics where others would blindly believe. You may use your Awareness Skill (usually with Wits for reactive and Resolve for conscious usage) to perceive things hidden by magic like latent and warding spells, users of Obfuscate, users of Presence or see through illusions like Chimerstry or Path of Illusion.</p>","src":{"book":"Dark Gifts: Disciplines Restored","page":"393"}},{"id":"FYu1PWlpi3gS54dl","n":"Overconfident","ed":"dgr","kind":"flaw","cat":"Mental","tag":"Flaw (••)","dots":[2],"sum":"Your confidence in yourself is unshakeable, but is so exaggerated that it makes working with others difficult. Benefitting or participating in Teamwork inflicts a point of Superficial Willpower damage.","html":"<p><em>Flaw (••)</em></p>\n<p>Your confidence in yourself is unshakeable, but is so exaggerated that it makes working with others difficult. Benefitting or participating in Teamwork inflicts a point of Superficial Willpower damage.</p>","src":{"book":"Dark Gifts: Disciplines Restored","page":"392"}},{"id":"G0EIf5oV49Dd8bf1","n":"Concentration","ed":"dgr","kind":"merit","cat":"Mental","tag":"Merit (•)","dots":[1],"sum":"You have the ability to focus your mind and shut out any distractions or annoyances.","html":"<p><em>Merit (•)</em></p>\n<p>You have the ability to focus your mind and shut out any distractions or annoyances. Characters with this Merit are unaffected by any penalties stemming from distracting circumstances (e.g., loud noises, hanging upside down) and distraction maneuver.</p>","src":{"book":"Dark Gifts: Disciplines Restored","page":"392"}},{"id":"GABaCv8DI9qYKRLL","n":"Sanguinary Overload (Quietus)","ed":"dgr","kind":"flaw","cat":"Ingrained Discipline","tag":"Flaw (•)","dots":[1],"sum":"You have become so attuned to reading the vagaries of blood that during feeding, the flood of information and voices from the victim cannot be avoided, even persisting beyond the hunt.","html":"<p><em>Flaw (•)</em></p>\n<p>You have become so attuned to reading the vagaries of blood that during feeding, the flood of information and voices from the victim cannot be avoided, even persisting beyond the hunt. For any scene/up to one hour (whichever is shorter) following a feeding, the vampire loses two dice from all Physical (Quietus Cruciatus) Social (Quietus Animae) or Mental (Sanguinis) pools due to the distraction. This Ingrained Flaw may be taken 3 times, each time corresponding to one of the forms of Quietus. Spending a point of Willpower allows the vampire to ignore this flaw for a turn.</p>","src":{"book":"Dark Gifts: Disciplines Restored","page":"389"}},{"id":"Hqgaotec0c4j0w2w","n":"Cainite Fangs","ed":"dgr","kind":"merit","cat":"Ghoul and Revenant","tag":"Merit (••)","dots":[2],"sum":"Through strange thaumaturgical sorcery, unknown rituals, Vicissitude or even an odd quirk of your physiology, your blood has been imbued with the power of the supernatural.","html":"<p><em>Merit (••)</em></p>\n<p>Through strange thaumaturgical sorcery, unknown rituals, Vicissitude or even an odd quirk of your physiology, your blood has been imbued with the power of the supernatural. You’ve grown retractable fangs like a vampire and may perform Bite Attacks like them, dealing flat two points of Aggravated Health damage even to supernatural beings after a successful called shot attack. Potential for feeding on vampires in the middle of combat is an ironic one.</p>\n<p>If the character acquires Distressing Fangs or Unretractable Fangs Flaw, the fangs may no longer be retractable. Cainite Fangs does not give the ability to give the Dark Kiss.</p>","src":{"book":"Dark Gifts: Disciplines Restored","page":"386"}},{"id":"INiT7w8ydJ9H0lty","n":"Blind","ed":"dgr","kind":"flaw","cat":"Physical","tag":"Flaw (•••)","dots":[3],"sum":"You cannot see. Characters can compensate for the loss of vision by becoming more sensitive to other sensory input, but visual cues and images are lost to them.","html":"<p><em>Flaw (•••)</em></p>\n<p>You cannot see. Characters can compensate for the loss of vision by becoming more sensitive to other sensory input, but visual cues and images are lost to them. Actions involving hand-eye coordination are very difficult to perform, especially under stressful conditions. Make vision-related rolls (and most combat rolls) at a -3 dice penalty.</p>\n<p>Vampires with Auspex and other creatures with supernatural senses are still able to use their powers (perhaps even to partially compensate for their loss of vision), though the information is interpreted via other senses.</p>","src":{"book":"Dark Gifts: Disciplines Restored","page":"394"}},{"id":"J629mvt4cFmu7UzH","n":"Incantation (Mortal Only)","ed":"dgr","kind":"merit","cat":"Magic","tag":"Merit (•–•••••)","dots":[1,2,3,4,5],"sum":"Incantations are the remnants of magic left in the world that the wise people or the old ladies in the woods know.","html":"<p><em>Merit (•–•••••)</em></p>\n<p>Incantations are the remnants of magic left in the world that the wise people or the old ladies in the woods know. Bits and pieces of world’s lore collected allow such powers to be passed from one generation to the next, usually verbally as many of its preactitioners are illiterate. Incantations are not nearly as flexible as Path-based or awakened magic known by sorcerers and witches or even the true magick known by will-working mages. Think of the old Egyptian amulets, the voodoo dolls sold to tourists and old curses levied by your babushas when you anger them.</p>\n<p>Each time this Background is bought, a mortal learns a Ritual (or a spell performed as a Ritual like Conveyance Path’s Spell). The Background’s rating is equal to the level of the Ritual (which is equal to the highest rank of the highest Aspect that the Incantation may use). For example, picking Aggressive Corpse (a lvl 3 Special Effect of Necromancy Path) as a 5-dot Incantation allows a mortal to perform a ritual to raise Aggressive Corpses with the limit on the Aspects being 4 (so Aspects like Duration, Number of Targets may be set up to 4)</p>\n<p>Incantations follow the rules of Hedge Magic (p. XX) for activation and rolls (default is Intelligence + Occult). A Special Effect or a Path spell like of Conveyance may be learned as a Ritual. Highest usable Aspect rank is equal to the The Merit may be picked normally at character creation to represent one’s heritage, but after that they must be learned as normal Rituals (same project as learning a normal Ritual, requiring two times as many total number of successes). Embrace fully refunds all Incantations as Incantations lack a linear structure to transfer knowledge into Blood Magic unlike Hedge Magic.</p>","src":{"book":"Dark Gifts: Disciplines Restored","page":"390"}},{"id":"K6Iq4Z3Dxd8bL6JW","n":"Agoraphobia (Flight)","ed":"dgr","kind":"flaw","cat":"Ingrained Discipline","tag":"Flaw (•)","dots":[1],"sum":"The vampire has spendt so much time flying safely that they no longer feel safe on the ground and may even struggle with moving on it.","html":"<p><em>Flaw (•)</em></p>\n<p>The vampire has spendt so much time flying safely that they no longer feel safe on the ground and may even struggle with moving on it. The vampire’s Frenzy Difficulties are increased by one while in an enclosed space where they have no ability to soar into the sky. The benefit of this Ingrained Flaw is that the user counts their Flight’s rating as one higher, up to 5, as the Discipline only has a single thing to offer per level.</p>","src":{"book":"Dark Gifts: Disciplines Restored","page":"389"}},{"id":"MHU4xfTrqNInuIjy","n":"Mute","ed":"dgr","kind":"flaw","cat":"Physical","tag":"Flaw (••)","dots":[2],"sum":"You cannot speak.","html":"<p><em>Flaw (••)</em></p>\n<p>You cannot speak. You may communicate with the Storyteller and describe your actions, but you cannot talk to player or Storyteller characters unless everyone concerned uses Linguistics dots to purchase a commonly understood sign language or you write down what you wish to say.</p>\n<p>Alternatively, this may be a Mental or a Social Flaw.</p>","src":{"book":"Dark Gifts: Disciplines Restored","page":"395"}},{"id":"MaTRWOLrbLYb5lNL","n":"Elderly (Mortal only)","ed":"dgr","kind":"flaw","cat":"Physical","tag":"Flaw (•••)","dots":[3],"sum":"Your body is frail and appears elderly whether as a result of aging or magic. Lose a die on all physical rolls. Ghouls and revenants may not take this Flaw. If a person with Elderly becomes a ghoul, they ignore the penalty.","html":"<p><em>Flaw (•••)</em></p>\n<p>Your body is frail and appears elderly whether as a result of aging or magic. Lose a die on all physical rolls. Ghouls and revenants may not take this Flaw. If a person with Elderly becomes a ghoul, they ignore the penalty.</p>","src":{"book":"Dark Gifts: Disciplines Restored","page":"394"}},{"id":"NbZEGz0xfYH40QQ9","n":"Revenant Heritage (Vampire)","ed":"dgr","kind":"merit","cat":"Supernatural","tag":"Merit (•)","dots":[1],"sum":"Despite your “ascension” over your revenant family members by acquiring the gift of vampirism, you have retained your old powers and many habits. Select one of the revenant families.","html":"<p><em>Merit (•)</em></p>\n<p>Despite your “ascension” over your revenant family members by acquiring the gift of vampirism, you have retained your old powers and many habits. Select one of the revenant families. You have access to its revenant loresheet and may even swap your in-Clan discipline spread for that of the revenant line. However, the Affliction that comes with your line is also present within you and you suffer the Family Affliction of the revenant line you have selected.</p>","src":{"book":"Dark Gifts: Disciplines Restored","page":"396"}},{"id":"Pa2IURl11noaWn5I","n":"Huge Size","ed":"dgr","kind":"merit","cat":"Physical","tag":"Merit (••••)","dots":[4],"sum":"You are abnormally large in size, at least six-foot-ten an 300 pounds in weight. Aside from making you extremely noticeable in public, this extra mass bestows an additional point of Health.","html":"<p><em>Merit (••••)</em></p>\n<p>You are abnormally large in size, at least six-foot-ten an 300 pounds in weight. Aside from making you extremely noticeable in public, this extra mass bestows an additional point of Health. Characters with this Merit also count their Strength as higher by one for feats of strength and gain a die certain strength-realted t actions: push objects, open barred doors, avoid being knocked down and so on. This die bonus is never used on offensive dice pools in combat.</p>\n<p>A person with Huge Size cannot take Everyman Looks Merit.</p>","src":{"book":"Dark Gifts: Disciplines Restored","page":"394"}},{"id":"QKKVd9ON7TUmxxJl","n":"Lame","ed":"dgr","kind":"flaw","cat":"Physical","tag":"Flaw (••••)","dots":[4],"sum":"Your legs are damaged, which prevents you from running or walking easily. You are forced to walk with a cane or possibly leg braces, and you have a pronounced lim to your stride.","html":"<p><em>Flaw (••••)</em></p>\n<p>Your legs are damaged, which prevents you from running or walking easily. You are forced to walk with a cane or possibly leg braces, and you have a pronounced lim to your stride. Your walking speed is one-quarter that of a normal human, and running is impossible. You suffer a three-dice penalty to all rolls involving your legs.</p>","src":{"book":"Dark Gifts: Disciplines Restored","page":"394"}},{"id":"RPw4kyeUrs25j9nc","n":"Killer Instinct (Potence)","ed":"dgr","kind":"flaw","cat":"Ingrained Discipline","tag":"Flaw (•)","dots":[1],"sum":"When the blood’s up, the echo of the Beast is never far away, and the unholy strength of Potence only serves to feed it.","html":"<p><em>Flaw (•)</em></p>\n<p>When the blood’s up, the echo of the Beast is never far away, and the unholy strength of Potence only serves to feed it. If the character fails a Rouse Check when activating a Potence power, they must immediately test for Fury Frenzy as if physically provoked.</p>","src":{"book":"Dark Gifts: Disciplines Restored","page":"389"}},{"id":"RQp4ve5WLYPn8rvo","n":"Lepper","ed":"dgr","kind":"flaw","cat":"Physical","tag":"Flaw (••–•••)","dots":[2,3],"sum":"Before you were Embraced, you suffered from leprosy or a skin disease that might be easily confused with leprosy. You cannot benefit from Looks Merits, and your flesh is marred by rashes, pale patches, festering sores, scars and so on.","html":"<p><em>Flaw (••–•••)</em></p>\n<p>Before you were Embraced, you suffered from leprosy or a skin disease that might be easily confused with leprosy. You cannot benefit from Looks Merits, and your flesh is marred by rashes, pale patches, festering sores, scars and so on. Your skin disease, whatever it actually is, is obvious to any who look upon you. Mortal authorities refuse you entry to any city, and you are treated with fear and revulsion. You may be compelled to dress in a specific manner and even carry castanets or other devices to announce your coming. In the three-point version, your condition is still contagious. Although it cannot have any further effects on your unliving form, you may transmit it to those upon whom you feed.</p>\n<p>This Flaw is worth only one point to Nosferatu and the Troglodytes, and then only if their disease is contagious.</p>","src":{"book":"Dark Gifts: Disciplines Restored","page":"395"}},{"id":"SDOJrcO4vvBDqBmw","n":"Small","ed":"dgr","kind":"flaw","cat":"Physical","tag":"Flaw (••)","dots":[2],"sum":"You are well below average height — four feet tall or less. You have difficulty reaching or manipulating objects designed for normal adult size, and your running speed is one-half that of a normally proportioned human.","html":"<p><em>Flaw (••)</em></p>\n<p>You are well below average height — four feet tall or less. You have difficulty reaching or manipulating objects designed for normal adult size, and your running speed is one-half that of a normally proportioned human.</p>\n<p>A person with Huge Size must take Everyday man Looks Merit.</p>","src":{"book":"Dark Gifts: Disciplines Restored","page":"395"}},{"id":"SY13wc2m0fCLdTld","n":"Stuff of Legends (Mytherceria)","ed":"dgr","kind":"flaw","cat":"Ingrained Discipline","tag":"Flaw (•)","dots":[1],"sum":"The vampire has used the fae-gift Discipline so much that they have begun to take on more and more characteristics of the fae. The vampire gains a number of Folkloric Constraints equal to their Mytherceria rating.","html":"<p><em>Flaw (•)</em></p>\n<p>The vampire has used the fae-gift Discipline so much that they have begun to take on more and more characteristics of the fae. The vampire gains a number of Folkloric Constraints equal to their Mytherceria rating.</p>","src":{"book":"Dark Gifts: Disciplines Restored","page":"389"}},{"id":"T86Oeq4LcJNUzWpd","n":"Ever-Shifting (Vicissitude)","ed":"dgr","kind":"flaw","cat":"Ingrained Discipline","tag":"Flaw (•)","dots":[1],"sum":"Those who have altered their undead flesh often find that it has gains some of…“sentience” of its own. The vampire must spend a Rouse Check of blood to calm it down until they next awaken or risk being a walking masquerade breach.","html":"<p><em>Flaw (•)</em></p>\n<p>Those who have altered their undead flesh often find that it has gains some of…“sentience” of its own. The vampire must spend a Rouse Check of blood to calm it down until they next awaken or risk being a walking masquerade breach. Uncalmed flesh also inflicts a penalty of two dice on the vampire’s Charisma and Seduction rolls.</p>","src":{"book":"Dark Gifts: Disciplines Restored","page":"390"}},{"id":"TIAOoLdWT4RbaGJU","n":"All is Maya (Chimerstry)","ed":"dgr","kind":"flaw","cat":"Ingrained Discipline","tag":"Flaw (•)","dots":[1],"sum":"Usage of Chimerstry stems from a tradition that the world is a “maya” – an illusion. The power itself lends credence to that idea as the users of Chimerstry can sometimes fail to differentiate between what is an illusion they have crafted.","html":"<p><em>Flaw (•)</em></p>\n<p>Usage of Chimerstry stems from a tradition that the world is a “maya” – an illusion. The power itself lends credence to that idea as the users of Chimerstry can sometimes fail to differentiate between what is an illusion they have crafted. The vampire suffers a dice pool to all Awareness and perception rolls equal to half of their rating Chimerstry rating, rounded up.</p>","src":{"book":"Dark Gifts: Disciplines Restored","page":"388"}},{"id":"UDbEodrMVICYYDNe","n":"Detached","ed":"dgr","kind":"merit","cat":"Numen","tag":"Merit (•••)","dots":[3],"sum":"You have the rare ability to view your psychic abilities as separate from your physical existence and can maintain this distance even under adverse conditions.","html":"<p><em>Merit (•••)</em></p>\n<p>You have the rare ability to view your psychic abilities as separate from your physical existence and can maintain this distance even under adverse conditions. Mental Impairment and penalties to the mind do not impact your Psychic Phenomena rolls. If you’re Incapacitated, then you may spend a Willpower point to use a Psychic Numina power at half the normal dice pool (rounded up). This action may be performed only once per scene, after which the character falls unconscious, so choose last-ditch efforts carefully.</p>","src":{"book":"Dark Gifts: Disciplines Restored","page":"393"}},{"id":"Y0wtOxMoXsLmNA7o","n":"Common Sense","ed":"dgr","kind":"merit","cat":"Mental","tag":"Merit (•)","dots":[1],"sum":"You have a significant amount of practical, everyday wisdom. Whenever the character is about to act in a way contrary to common sense, the Storyteller can make suggestions or warnings about the implications of said action.","html":"<p><em>Merit (•)</em></p>\n<p>You have a significant amount of practical, everyday wisdom. Whenever the character is about to act in a way contrary to common sense, the Storyteller can make suggestions or warnings about the implications of said action. This is a very useful Merit to give to beginning players who are unfamiliar with the game.</p>","src":{"book":"Dark Gifts: Disciplines Restored","page":"391"}},{"id":"YcRs6gud6ZY1w6PQ","n":"Retainer","ed":"dgr","kind":"background","cat":"Backgrounds","tag":"","dots":[],"sum":"You have one or more loyal and steadfast servants or assistants.","html":"<p>You have one or more loyal and steadfast servants or assistants. Retainers may be ghouls who are blood bound to you, individuals you have so Dominated over the years that they are incapable of independent action, or individuals you have so overwhelmed with your Presence that they would do anything for you.</p>\n<p>You must always control retainers in some fashion: a salary, vitae donation, or direct mesmerism. Though typically loyal, retainers may betray you if the reward outweighs the risk or if you have treated them badly. The Storyteller can always call for a scene between you and a retainer.</p>\n<p>You may not directly benefit from Advantages of your Retainers without the permission of the Storyteller in rare cases. You may not feed on your Retainers for free as Herd exists to represent that. Retainers are characters, not puppets, even though players may play them to ease the Storyteller’s burden. At Storyteller’s discretion, players can improve their Retainers by paying for things like Specialties, Disciplines and Numina with their character’s own experience points cost, but they may not improve their Attributes or Skills directly.</p>\n<p>Retainers should act as characters, not puppets. Even ghoul retainers aren’t supermen; the most skilled servants don’t always show the highest loyalty. Everything in Vampire is a tradeoff. Storytellers can use Retainers to add flavor to the chronicle; don’t let them or their misuse damage the story. If the Storyteller is fine with it, they may let a player directly control and roleplay the Retainer character.</p>\n<p>Most retainers, including non-human ones, use human NPC traits (p. XX) to make things easier for both the players and the Storytellers.</p>\n<h3>Human Retainer</h3>\n<p>You either employ a human to work for you or, if you are a vampire, you probably ghoul them. While not as loyal as blood bound ones, paid mortals are loyal enough as long as the coin flows. The maximum number of Human Retainers is equal to one’s Resources + Business Establishment to represent the ability to upkeep them which is especially especially important for non-vampire players. Blood bound Retainers do not count towards this limit.</p>\n<p>If you turn the human retainer into a ghoul, see ghoul mechanics on p. XX.</p>\n<p>• A Weak Individual.</p>\n<p>•• An Average Individual.</p>\n<p>••• A Gifted Individual.</p>\n<h3>Animal Companion</h3>\n<p>You have managed to befriend and train an animal that follows you around and obeys your orders as much as it is able.</p>\n<p>You need to possess at least Animal Ken 3 in order to purchase this Background. An animal may become a ghoul (see p. XX).</p>\n<p>The maximum number of Animal Companion dots a character may possess in total is equal to one’s Animalism (+ Power Bonus)/Animal Ken + Kennel Haven Background. Ghouled animals do not count towards this limit. Famuli and familairs are not counted as Animal Companions since they are bought together with powers and rituals.</p>\n<p>• Medium/Small: One medium (wolf, large dog, boar, large snake) animal or three small (bird, lizard, snake, cat) animals.</p>\n<p>•• Large: One large animal (a bear, crocodile, lion, cow) or a swarm</p>\n<p>••• Swarm: A swarm of small animals (bats, snakes, bees...)</p>\n<h3>Bound Umbral Spirit</h3>\n<p>Through deals, appeasements or magic dealing with spirits, you have a spirit of the umbra in your service. Usually, this Background must be justified through knowledge of a Path of magic or a Discipline that deals with such spirits or story encounters. Spirits represented here are from a weaker stock of spirits. Acquiring an Umbral Spirit Retainer requires either their consent, usually done through pacts, or knowing how to bind them (such as through the Path of Binding, Summoning and Warding). Advantages are not necessary when building your spirit retainers.</p>\n<p>A retainer gains 15 times its dots in experience points to spend on Spirit Powers and traits for free.</p>\n<p>•• Small Spirit: Build them as a Weak Individual.</p>\n<p>••• Weak Spirit: Build them as an Average Individual.</p>\n<p>•••• Average Spirit: Build them as an Average Individual.</p>\n<p>••••• Powerful Spirit: Build them as a Gifted Individual. Powerful Spirits are rare and not available unless earned in-game.</p>\n<h3>Ghost Retainer</h3>\n<p>You have enlisted or enslaved a ghost which obeys your commands without much hesitation.</p>\n<p>One may think that they do not need to buy a ghost servant as they could keep spamming Compel Soul or threatening their fetters for obedience. That one would, however, find that their haven was set on fire either by the vengeful ghost or their allies while they slept. Buying this Background ensures some safety and/or loyalty for the necromancer.</p>\n<p>This Background can only be bought by ghost characters or by characters who have powers that would allow them to communicate with or enslave ghosts. After character creation, a character will require a ghost’s fetter or permission to purchase them. For portraying ghosts in a simple manner, see p. XX.</p>\n<p>The Ghost Retainer gains experience points equal to 5 times the dot rating of the ghost to spend on Arcanoi powers and other traits (like Plasma Rating) of the purchased ghost. Ghost Retainers begin with Plasma rating 1.</p>\n<p>•• Weak Ghost: Build them as a Weak Individual.</p>\n<p>••• Average Ghost: Build them like an Average Individual.</p>\n<p>•••• Seasoned Ghost: Stats of a Gifted Individual.</p>\n<p>••••• Powerful Ghost: Build them as a Superlative Individual. These ghosts are rare and only given by the Storyteller.</p>\n<h3>Necromantic Construct</h3>\n<p>Necromancers frequently make zombies, various small homunculi and other undead creatures to keep them company, have them act as soldiers or fulfill some other menial tasks that they can’t be bothered with. Necromantic Construct Background serves to solidify your permanent hold on the construct created by the Discipline of Necromancy as otherwise, as stated within individual powers and Rituals, these constructs tend to go rogue or fall apart unless bought.</p>\n<p>See Lesser Undead for Necromantic Construct costs.</p>\n<h3>Revenant Retainer</h3>\n<p>Revenants are the products of profane magics and centuries of eugenics programs that crossbred ghouls in order to create self-sustaining ghouls that could be more independent. These revenants were organised in family lines and most of them can trace back their origins to the Tzimisce, but not all. For how revenants work and their families, see p. XX.</p>\n<p>Unless they are a revenant themselves, a character may possess one revenant retainer per point of Influence among their family line or relevant sect or clan status (for example, Tzimisce for Zantosa or True Black Hand for Enrathi). The exception to this are revenants acquired through loresheets.</p>\n<p>•• Weak Revenant: Build them as a Weak Individual</p>\n<p>••• Average Revenant: Build them as an Average Individual</p>\n<p>•••• Gifted Revenant: Build them as a Gifted Individual</p>\n<h3>Undead’s Familiar (Vampire only)</h3>\n<p>While the Embrace destroyed your ability to function as an awakened mage, your bond with your familiar companion endures. The familiar is a spirit of high intelligence, able to telepathically commune with others or talk normally. It may take a form of a mouse, a cat or another animal the size of a dog or smaller that is often associated with practitioners of magic. It may not be a magical creature (dragons, imps, etc.), but creatures such as a homunculus, zombie or animated bodyparts might work well. The form the familiar takes must make sense for the former mage. For example, a character connected with nature and the living world would not keep a zombie or an animated skull as a familiar. All familiars have their own personalities and secret agendas which not even mind-reading or fortune-telling magicians can divine.</p>\n<p>Familiars are immune to mind influencing and dominating powers such as Dominate, Dementation and Presence but are fully vulnerable to effects that would harm spirits such as Ward against Spirits.</p>\n<p>Familiars may provide valuable advice as more often than not they know more than their master on the matters of magic. When learning Blood Magic Paths and Rituals, a Familiar may provide teamwork as well if it possesses at least two dots into Occult. Familiars will always sense the current emotions of their master, rushing to aid if they are willing to help. Characters who gain another familiar (such as by Blood Magic rituals) cut their connection with this one as the former-mage familiar will feel betrayed, replaced and highly insulted.</p>\n<p>This Background is available only to characters who possessed a familiar before the Embrace and may be purchased only once. A destroyed familiar reconstitutes itself after a story and returns to its master, continuing its duties. Familiars also crave gifts, favors, affection, and so on. The nature of those offerings depends on the individual familiar and their relationship with their master and might be weird, arbitrary, or outright horrifying.</p>\n<p>Familiars are built like Mortals stat-wise, but you swap their Physical Attributes for that of the animal. Mortal stats are that of the spirit, animal’s are the innate abilities and instincts of the host body. Familairs also possess two dots in a magical Path.</p>\n<p>••• Flip a coin/roll one die. If the coin does not land on the called side/the die fails, the familiar will not carry out your request unless somehow forced (and they will not do a very good job). Build them as a Weak Individual.</p>\n<p>•••• Your familiar will perform complex tasks tasks that will not endanger its own existence unless the master’s existence is at stake. Build them as an Average Individual.</p>\n<p>••••• Your familiar is fully obedient to you and will do whatever you ask to it. Build them as a Gifted Individual.</p>\n<h3>Retainer Flaws</h3>\n<p>• Stalkers. You have a tendency to attract people who become a tad too smitten with you for your own good. A former retainer retains their memory of you and a desire to reconnect. They may be hungry, love-maddened, desperate, opportunistic, or any combination or variation. Should you get rid of them, another soon appears.</p>","src":{"book":"Dark Gifts: Disciplines Restored","page":"383"}},{"id":"Z1JrqeKESr10TwUK","n":"Blood Empathy (Ghoul)","ed":"dgr","kind":"merit","cat":"Ghoul and Revenant","tag":"Merit (••)","dots":[2],"sum":"The ghoul can feel if their regnant is in danger or otherwise, needs them immediately, this does not allow for telepathic communication.","html":"<p><em>Merit (••)</em></p>\n<p>The ghoul can feel if their regnant is in danger or otherwise, needs them immediately, this does not allow for telepathic communication.</p>","src":{"book":"Dark Gifts: Disciplines Restored","page":"386"}},{"id":"ZWjKLfcBsoKOxtap","n":"Distressing Fangs","ed":"dgr","kind":"flaw","cat":"Ghoul and Revenant","tag":"Flaw (•)","dots":[1],"sum":"Having developed fangs like a vampire, the character suffers one die on Social pools with mundane mortals.","html":"<p><em>Flaw (•)</em></p>\n<p>Having developed fangs like a vampire, the character suffers one die on Social pools with mundane mortals.</p>","src":{"book":"Dark Gifts: Disciplines Restored","page":"387"}},{"id":"Zgsz2eeq52pVHzxw","n":"Breakdown (Celerity)","ed":"dgr","kind":"flaw","cat":"Ingrained Discipline","tag":"Flaw (•)","dots":[1],"sum":"The vampire’s physiology runs at the same pace as the vampire, burning blood and energy at equal volumes.","html":"<p><em>Flaw (•)</em></p>\n<p>The vampire’s physiology runs at the same pace as the vampire, burning blood and energy at equal volumes. Whenever the vampire fails a Rouse Check when activating a Celerity power, they sustain a point of Aggravated Health damage, and their body withers and ages before their very eyes.</p>","src":{"book":"Dark Gifts: Disciplines Restored","page":"388"}},{"id":"ZnZREcVndE1eYJZY","n":"Song Unending (Melpominee)","ed":"dgr","kind":"flaw","cat":"Ingrained Discipline","tag":"Flaw (•)","dots":[1],"sum":"The song heard by the Daughters of Cacophony and all the practitioners of this Discipline keeps being distracting and, unless you let it flow through you, you find it hard to express yourself.","html":"<p><em>Flaw (•)</em></p>\n<p>The song heard by the Daughters of Cacophony and all the practitioners of this Discipline keeps being distracting and, unless you let it flow through you, you find it hard to express yourself. Lose three dice on all Social Skill tests if you aren’t utilising Performance, poetry or music of some kind.</p>","src":{"book":"Dark Gifts: Disciplines Restored","page":"389"}},{"id":"Zv7NR0STOKWG7qYD","n":"Crone’s Curse","ed":"dgr","kind":"flaw","cat":"Physical","tag":"Flaw (••)","dots":[2],"sum":"The character appears at least two decades older than they actually are which reduces their Health tracker by one. This is usually the result of a curse levied by a witch, but ghouling and embrace can also inflict it.","html":"<p><em>Flaw (••)</em></p>\n<p>The character appears at least two decades older than they actually are which reduces their Health tracker by one. This is usually the result of a curse levied by a witch, but ghouling and embrace can also inflict it. Most notably, the embrace within Crone line of the Nosferatu.</p>","src":{"book":"Dark Gifts: Disciplines Restored","page":"394"}},{"id":"aVrW5niH9DKwTJb4","n":"Amnesia","ed":"dgr","kind":"flaw","cat":"Mental","tag":"Flaw (•)","dots":[1],"sum":"You are unable to remember anything about your past, yourself or your family, though your past might well come back to haunt you.","html":"<p><em>Flaw (•)</em></p>\n<p>You are unable to remember anything about your past, yourself or your family, though your past might well come back to haunt you. Your origins and the circumstances behind your amnesia are for the Storyteller to determine, and they encouraged to make it as interesting as possible.</p>","src":{"book":"Dark Gifts: Disciplines Restored","page":"392"}},{"id":"bS0ekyJMMSkzljmX","n":"Realisation (Daimonion)","ed":"dgr","kind":"flaw","cat":"Ingrained Discipline","tag":"Flaw (•)","dots":[1],"sum":"The suffering is everywhere in the world and no force can stop it, only master it.","html":"<p><em>Flaw (•)</em></p>\n<p>The suffering is everywhere in the world and no force can stop it, only master it. Those who feast themselves on Daimonion’s gifts see sin and suffering everywhere and they understand that their powers resemble the beasts already consigned to Hell at the day of judgement. While many lie to themselves, denying it or rationalising it, they are still reminded each time they gaze upon the world and what is has to offer. The vampire fulfilling their desire heals no Willpower as the user is in a perpetual existential crisis or in deep nihilism with or without their realisation.</p>","src":{"book":"Dark Gifts: Disciplines Restored","page":"388"}},{"id":"bsaWb3Y8N3YECsQC","n":"Misplaced Heart","ed":"dgr","kind":"merit","cat":"Physical","tag":"Merit (••)","dots":[2],"sum":"Your heart is elsewhere in your body, no more than 2 feet from its original position, due to various factors such as dextrocardia or other means such as uses of fleshcrafting. An additional success is required to aim fort yourt heart.","html":"<p><em>Merit (••)</em></p>\n<p>Your heart is elsewhere in your body, no more than 2 feet from its original position, due to various factors such as dextrocardia or other means such as uses of fleshcrafting. An additional success is required to aim fort yourt heart.</p>","src":{"book":"Dark Gifts: Disciplines Restored","page":"394"}},{"id":"cYt018AF33itz1J0","n":"Creature of Habit (Vampire)","ed":"dgr","kind":"flaw","cat":"Mental","tag":"Flaw (•)","dots":[1],"sum":"You always stake out the same places to hunt, night after night due to familiarity and comfortability. Due to this, feeding outside your usual haunts causes anxiety and discomfort.","html":"<p><em>Flaw (•)</em></p>\n<p>You always stake out the same places to hunt, night after night due to familiarity and comfortability. Due to this, feeding outside your usual haunts causes anxiety and discomfort. When feeding outside your Predator Type, increase the Hunting Difficulty by 2.</p>","src":{"book":"Dark Gifts: Disciplines Restored","page":"392"}},{"id":"eRbY913Bd17sXl7y","n":"Gifted Magician","ed":"dgr","kind":"merit","cat":"Magic","tag":"Merit (••••)","dots":[4],"sum":"Perhaps you browsed countless texts, conversed with the most learned of scholars or just possess a massive library you frequently spend hours in. Learning magic has become second nature to you.","html":"<p><em>Merit (••••)</em></p>\n<p>Perhaps you browsed countless texts, conversed with the most learned of scholars or just possess a massive library you frequently spend hours in. Learning magic has become second nature to you. You halve and round up all threshold Difficulties for learning or creating new magic. This does not stack with any similar reductions as they usually come from a different source such as better teaching materials. You are just better at it by yourself.</p>","src":{"book":"Dark Gifts: Disciplines Restored","page":"390"}},{"id":"fPpCnWuj8Ocs6NGk","n":"Celestial Attunement","ed":"dgr","kind":"merit","cat":"Mental","tag":"Merit (•)","dots":[1],"sum":"You have an innate link to the passage of time and the movement of celestial bodies. You can estimate the time until sunrise or sunset within a minute or two, and you can follow the phases of the moon in your head.","html":"<p><em>Merit (•)</em></p>\n<p>You have an innate link to the passage of time and the movement of celestial bodies. You can estimate the time until sunrise or sunset within a minute or two, and you can follow the phases of the moon in your head. Those with some training in astrology and this Merit can even foretell certain astrological conjunctions without access to charts. You can accomplish any of these feats with only a minimum of concentration.</p>","src":{"book":"Dark Gifts: Disciplines Restored","page":"391"}},{"id":"fVgu6c0IF0BHBOxe","n":"Stasis (Protean)","ed":"dgr","kind":"flaw","cat":"Ingrained Discipline","tag":"Flaw (•)","dots":[1],"sum":"The vampire’s static nature rebels against the forces of change.","html":"<p><em>Flaw (•)</em></p>\n<p>The vampire’s static nature rebels against the forces of change. If the vampire fails a Rouse Check while activating a Protean power, when they revert to their normal form the change is incomplete — dulled and useless claws persist, a foot remains stuck in the soil, or the fleshcrafter finds their skin attached to their subject. Unfinished transformations never provide any benefit. The vampire must remove the affected area (sustaining one Aggravated damage) or wait for it to revert (un)naturally during day-sleep.</p>","src":{"book":"Dark Gifts: Disciplines Restored","page":"389"}},{"id":"flFHJ5yI9xtiMekp","n":"Egomaniac (Presence)","ed":"dgr","kind":"flaw","cat":"Ingrained Discipline","tag":"Flaw (•)","dots":[1],"sum":"Having had others consistently fawn over you has stroked your ego to the point where you can’t tolerate a world without you at its center.","html":"<p><em>Flaw (•)</em></p>\n<p>Having had others consistently fawn over you has stroked your ego to the point where you can’t tolerate a world without you at its center. Whenever a Presence power generates a messy critical or fails to sway your target, you must make a Fury Frenzy test at Difficulty 2.</p>","src":{"book":"Dark Gifts: Disciplines Restored","page":"389"}},{"id":"hEvodBxUslAfPhTv","n":"Domain","ed":"dgr","kind":"background","cat":"Backgrounds","tag":"","dots":[],"sum":"Your have access to a site that lies over a place of strong magical power that resonates with your sorcery or enchanted it (rule of thumb is 1 year per dot of Sanctum).","html":"<h3>Sanctum</h3>\n<p>Your have access to a site that lies over a place of strong magical power that resonates with your sorcery or enchanted it (rule of thumb is 1 year per dot of Sanctum). While invisible to normal sight (though not perhaps to Auspex or various Rituals), it underlies a prominent area: the top of a staircase, a fountain in the garden, the center of a room. (Intelligence + Occult might allow someone to deduce its location, if they know what they’re looking for.)</p>\n<p>While within a Sanctum, sorcerers who practice the type of sorcery that resonates with it acquire benefits to casting and do not trigger magical risks on a Botch casting magic within its boundaries.</p>\n<p>When purchasing this Background, a character selects one type of a Sanctum. Each Sanctum has its own effects and types of magic it can benefit. Storytellers may experiment with their own types of Sancta and use the ones listed below as samples.</p>\n<p>• A tiny sanctum the size of a pantry or a small clearing; +1 die to all rolls to use magic while in your sanctum.</p>\n<p>•• A small room or copse of trees; +1 die to all rolls to use magic while in your sanctum.</p>\n<p>••• A workshop and perhaps an adjoining bedroom or closet; +2 dice to all rolls to use magic while in your sanctum.</p>\n<p>•••• A small house or cottage or a suite of rooms in a larger building; +2 dice to all rolls to use magic and -1 to casting Difficulty while in your sanctum.</p>\n<p>••••• An entire mansion or a large wing of a castle, or perhaps a forest. You immediately know of any incursion onto your lands unless they’re hidden supernaturally (you may roll Wits + Awareness + Sanctum to notice them against their power); +2 dice to all rolls to use magic and -1 to casting Difficulty while in your sanctum.</p>\n<p>Greater ratings and sizes are allowed at the Storyteller’s discretion. Sancta like Shrines may be portable. Detection through Sanctum is possible only if the magician attunes themselves to the place, usually by acting as its steward or owner.</p>\n<p>Types of Sancta are:</p>\n<p>• Glyphs of Power: One of the more common Sancta, Glyphs of Power is a location in which magical energy has been stored by those who can wield it, inscribing the very vis into the area through glyphs, art or some other auspicious elements.</p>\n<p>When purchasing this Sanctum Background, select one sorcery school. The benefits of Sanctum will only affect it and potentially sycrethic sorceries. For example, sorcery of Celtic Blood Witchcraft might interact with that of druid hedge wizards or magi. Some say that Stonehendge is not a Natural Sanctum, but one created by ancient Celtic magi. Tremere are particularly inclined to this type of a Sanctum as it allows them to not rely on locations where others already dwell, even though it can require anywhere from several years to centuries to inscribe proper Glyphs of Power onto a location.</p>\n<p>• Natural Sanctum: A naturally occuring Sanctum, rich in vis (later known as Quintessence). Some call them Caerns, others call the knots or forks in the veins of the Earth. It benefits all magic.</p>\n<p>• Weak Veil: Either due to an accident, artefacts, frequency of dark rituals or due to a natural phenomenon, the Sanctum is a location where the Veil that separates the lands of the living and the lands of the dead is thin.</p>\n<p>This Type of a Sanctum benefits necromantic schools such as Mortis and Nihilistics. Necromantic Paths from other Schools like Blood Witchcraft and Akhu may also benefit from this Sanctum. Shroud rating of this location is normally Frayed, and ghosts frequent it unless necromancers hostile to them hold the place.</p>\n<p>• Shrine: Shrine is a constructed Sanctum powering religious magic such as Akhu, certain sects of Dur-An-Ki, Mortis, Blood Witchcraft, and even Shalimite Abyss Mysticism. When purchasing Shrine, select a deity. Those who believe in the power of that deity or a religion receive the Sanctum bonus for that magic. For example, a lector priest of Akhu might have Shrine (Set) or Shrine (Echidna), while a practitioner of Dur-An-Ki could could have a shrine dedicated to an ancient god or even a religion such as Shrine (Islam).</p>\n<p>At greater sizes, Shrine is also often referred to as holy/unholy/sanctified grounds.</p>\n<p>■ <strong>Spirit Presence:</strong> The area is busy with</p>\n<p>• activity of spirits and is usually found in nature. Spirit Presence benefits magics based on spirits such as Koldunism and Dur- An-Ki. At Storyteller’s discretion, other practices and Paths could benefit as well from interacting with spirits in the area.</p>","src":{"book":"Dark Gifts: Disciplines Restored","page":"379"}},{"id":"hyoVzOl8UQbn33yI","n":"Coldy Logical","ed":"dgr","kind":"merit","cat":"Mental","tag":"Merit (•)","dots":[1],"sum":"While some might refer to you as a “cold fish,” you have a knack for separating factual reporting from emotional or hysterical coloration.","html":"<p><em>Merit (•)</em></p>\n<p>While some might refer to you as a “cold fish,” you have a knack for separating factual reporting from emotional or hysterical coloration. You are probably not very emotional yourself, but you can see clearly when others are clouding the facts with their feelings. Gain a die bonus to all Insight rolls and rolls to perceive and anylse feelings, but lose a die to Charisma or Persuasion rolls.</p>","src":{"book":"Dark Gifts: Disciplines Restored","page":"391"}},{"id":"iP9U3AEUhpklOye0","n":"Resistant to Mind-Bending","ed":"dgr","kind":"merit","cat":"Supernatural","tag":"Merit (•••)","dots":[3],"sum":"Your will is that of iron. Gain a two-dice bonus to any roll to resist an effect to sway or bend your will (Dominate, Presence, etc.) effect once per session.","html":"<p><em>Merit (•••)</em></p>\n<p>Your will is that of iron. Gain a two-dice bonus to any roll to resist an effect to sway or bend your will (Dominate, Presence, etc.) effect once per session. Should you acquire a dot in such Disciplines or acquire powers to sway minds of others, you lose the benefit of this Merit and it is refunded.</p>\n<p>Whenever somebody is using a mind-swaing effect on you, you may roll Wits + Awareness vs. Wits + Subterfuge. On a win, you become aware of it.</p>","src":{"book":"Dark Gifts: Disciplines Restored","page":"396"}},{"id":"jRhXUJa0uU4rMRZ9","n":"Unretractable Fangs (Vampire only)","ed":"dgr","kind":"flaw","cat":"Physical","tag":"Flaw (•)","dots":[1],"sum":"Not being able to retract their fangs, the character is quite scary whether they’re smiling with visible fangs or weirdly not smilling. The character suffers a die penalty to Social pools with mundane mortals.","html":"<p><em>Flaw (•)</em></p>\n<p></p>\n<p>Not being able to retract their fangs, the character is quite scary whether they’re smiling with visible fangs or weirdly not smilling. The character suffers a die penalty to Social pools with mundane mortals. Nagaraja may not take this Flaw as it is a part of their Bane already. Ghoul variant of this is called Distressing Fangs.</p>","src":{"book":"Dark Gifts: Disciplines Restored","page":"395"}},{"id":"kEUaipV8iq5eUvKo","n":"Magically Inept","ed":"dgr","kind":"flaw","cat":"Magic","tag":"Flaw (•••)","dots":[3],"sum":"Known as Thaumaturgically Inept in Tremere lexicon, you simply do not click with magic. Difficulties on casting tests are raised by 1 for hedge sorcery, sphere magic, pillar magic and blood magic.","html":"<p><em>Flaw (•••)</em></p>\n<p>Known as Thaumaturgically Inept in Tremere lexicon, you simply do not click with magic. Difficulties on casting tests are raised by 1 for hedge sorcery, sphere magic, pillar magic and blood magic. Can only be taken by those who have at least one dot in sorcery, magick spheres/pillars or have Blood Sorcery as an-in Clan Discipline.</p>","src":{"book":"Dark Gifts: Disciplines Restored","page":"391"}},{"id":"mHaZ7gNVFlWADT8r","n":"Decay (Thanatosis)","ed":"dgr","kind":"flaw","cat":"Ingrained Discipline","tag":"Flaw (•)","dots":[1],"sum":"The powers of Thanatosis allow its user to debilitate and decay the flesh of their enemies, but its entropic energies reflect onto the casters when overused. The vampire counts their Strength as lower by two, to a minimum of 1.","html":"<p><em>Flaw (•)</em></p>\n<p>The powers of Thanatosis allow its user to debilitate and decay the flesh of their enemies, but its entropic energies reflect onto the casters when overused. The vampire counts their Strength as lower by two, to a minimum of 1. This may be mitigated for a scene by spending a Rouse Check to renew the flesh.</p>","src":{"book":"Dark Gifts: Disciplines Restored","page":"390"}},{"id":"nPBsKJHsb4ldcVHe","n":"Resistant to Pyshics","ed":"dgr","kind":"merit","cat":"Supernatural","tag":"Merit (•••)","dots":[3],"sum":"Psychic Numina have little effect on you and you have little effect on it. Gain a two-dice bonus to any roll to resist any Psychic Phenomenon power or that of Auspex effect once per session.","html":"<p><em>Merit (•••)</em></p>\n<p>Psychic Numina have little effect on you and you have little effect on it. Gain a two-dice bonus to any roll to resist any Psychic Phenomenon power or that of Auspex effect once per session. Should you acquire a dot in any Psychic Phenomenon or Auspex, you lose the benefit of this Merit and it is refunded.</p>\n<p>Whenever somebody is using a Psychic Phenomenon or scrying effect on you, you may roll Wits + Awareness vs. Wits + Subterfuge. On a win, you become aware of it.</p>","src":{"book":"Dark Gifts: Disciplines Restored","page":"396"}},{"id":"o60I0jBsQyNSWDDr","n":"Pain Tolerance","ed":"dgr","kind":"merit","cat":"Physical","tag":"Merit (••)","dots":[2],"sum":"You are gifted with a tough body, deadened nerves, or some ability to block out pain. When a character is Impaired through damage to the Health tracker, they suffer a -1 penalty instead of the normal -2.","html":"<p><em>Merit (••)</em></p>\n<p>You are gifted with a tough body, deadened nerves, or some ability to block out pain. When a character is Impaired through damage to the Health tracker, they suffer a -1 penalty instead of the normal -2.</p>","src":{"book":"Dark Gifts: Disciplines Restored","page":"394"}},{"id":"oHMFqWJ9jlu9gUDw","n":"Laham","ed":"dgr","kind":"flaw","cat":"Infernal","tag":"Flaw (•–•••••)","dots":[1,2,3,4,5],"sum":"You belong to one of the bloodlines that literally descend from demons. Once called the Laham in ancient tongues, the term Nephilim is the more popular one recently.","html":"<p><em>Flaw (•–•••••)</em></p>\n<p>You belong to one of the bloodlines that literally descend from demons. Once called the Laham in ancient tongues, the term Nephilim is the more popular one recently. The Lahama tend to live to around 200 years unless slain prior as they age more slowly and more gracefully.</p>\n<p>You gain a number of Arcana dots equal to your dot rating in this Merit to spend on your demonic powers. They are not a part of any limit like that of thralls as they are your inborn gifts developed during teenhood or early adulthood. Additionally, you the first two dots of Divinity at character creation and may purchase more as normal. Your maximum Faith Exhastion is equal to your Divinity rating. For your powers, see p. XX The Lahama have a natural presence that is detectable by, demons, infernalists and other evil spirits. When engaging with such creatures in their infernal courts and institutions, count your Status as two higher with them, to a maximum of 5. Many Lahama were also sired by demons possessing mortals, but not all of them are human. Folk stories tell of monsters coming out of wombs with no human words to describe them. Some were even raised up by the Church as weapons against evil, but they are a double-edged weapon as nobody can be sure were their true loyalties lie.</p>\n<p>True Faith and aura-reading abilities like Scry the Soul may also detect Lahama.</p>","src":{"book":"Dark Gifts: Disciplines Restored","page":"387"}},{"id":"qNU1P3u83s1JaOvD","n":"Resistant to Magic","ed":"dgr","kind":"merit","cat":"Supernatural","tag":"Merit (•••)","dots":[3],"sum":"Magic has little effect on you and you have little effect on it. Gain a two-dice bonus to any roll to resist a magical (Blood Sorcery, psy, Pillars or Spheres of magi, Hedge Magic and so) effect once per session.","html":"<p><em>Merit (•••)</em></p>\n<p>Magic has little effect on you and you have little effect on it. Gain a two-dice bonus to any roll to resist a magical (Blood Sorcery, psy, Pillars or Spheres of magi, Hedge Magic and so) effect once per session. Should you acquire a dot in any sorcery, you lose the benefit of this Merit and it is refunded.</p>\n<p>Whenever somebody is using magic on you, you may roll Wits + Awareness vs. Wits + Subterfuge. On a win, you become aware of it.</p>","src":{"book":"Dark Gifts: Disciplines Restored","page":"396"}},{"id":"qjNNHOxZ5fg0IhUE","n":"Oracle","ed":"dgr","kind":"merit","cat":"Numen","tag":"Merit (••••)","dots":[4],"sum":"You can see and interpret signs and omens. You are able to draw advice from these omens, for they provide hints of the future and warnings of the present.","html":"<p><em>Merit (••••)</em></p>\n<p>You can see and interpret signs and omens. You are able to draw advice from these omens, for they provide hints of the future and warnings of the present. When the Storyteller feels that you are in position to see an omen, you will be required to make a Resolve + Awareness roll, with the difficulty relative to how well the omen is concealed. If successful, you may then roll Intelligence + Occult to interpret what you have seen; the difficulty is again relative to the complexity of the omen.</p>\n<p>Once per session, you may force an omen and roll Resolve + Awareness against a Difficulty of 3. On a win, roll Intelligence + Occult. Half the successes (rounded down) may be added as a dice bonus on non-tracker pools, but no more than three dice may be added per roll. Divination cannot aid pools to fortune-tell or where the user consciously forced some kind of a benefit (like from a Blood Surge, artefact usage or another dice bonus) as fate comes on its own and artefacts often influence change and fate by themselves.</p>","src":{"book":"Dark Gifts: Disciplines Restored","page":"393"}},{"id":"r1lxBtoA5I4wlTAT","n":"Vengeful","ed":"dgr","kind":"flaw","cat":"Mental","tag":"Flaw (••)","dots":[2],"sum":"You have a score to settle, incurred either during your mortal days or after the Embrace. You are obsessed with taking your revenge on an individual or group, and must make a Fury Frenzy (Difficulty 3) in their presence.","html":"<p><em>Flaw (••)</em></p>\n<p>You have a score to settle, incurred either during your mortal days or after the Embrace. You are obsessed with taking your revenge on an individual or group, and must make a Fury Frenzy (Difficulty 3) in their presence.</p>","src":{"book":"Dark Gifts: Disciplines Restored","page":"393"}},{"id":"sz3llTVzAfYc2gqB","n":"Untained (Thrall Only)","ed":"dgr","kind":"merit","cat":"Infernal","tag":"Merit (•••••)","dots":[5],"sum":"Your enthrallment did not stain your soul with the poison of Torment. Although you do not need to check for degeneration, you may still gain Torment from using corrupt Arcana that impose taint.","html":"<p><em>Merit (•••••)</em></p>\n<p>Your enthrallment did not stain your soul with the poison of Torment. Although you do not need to check for degeneration, you may still gain Torment from using corrupt Arcana that impose taint. If you ever accumulate a dot of permanent Torment, you lose this Merit. Thralls with this Merit can only receive Arcana by taking Taints and Pacts.</p>","src":{"book":"Dark Gifts: Disciplines Restored","page":"387"}},{"id":"t5jtTwArs4lLOHE1","n":"Magically Attuned","ed":"dgr","kind":"merit","cat":"Magic","tag":"Merit (•–••)","dots":[1,2],"sum":"All humans can use magic — if they are alive.","html":"<p><em>Merit (•–••)</em></p>\n<p>All humans can use magic — if they are alive. Upon being Embraced, a wizard loses all of their magic and must instead rework their practice through Blood Magic which is not as easy to learn as it requires proper resonance like all vampiric Disciplines and even ghouls who have no need for Resonance know that something is off when it comes to learning blood magic in contrast to common mortal margic.</p>\n<p>When you were embraced, you did not lose the ability to learn magic and the change onto Blood Magic was much smoother and you find little difference between the two when it comes to learning them. For one dot, you do not require Resonance to advance through Blood Sorcery Discipline. At two dots, you may learn Blood Sorcery Discipline at a cost of 6 x the new level, unless it costs less due to being in-clan Discipline or from other factors. For mortals, this makes the cost of Hedge Sorcery Schools 5 x new level.</p>","src":{"book":"Dark Gifts: Disciplines Restored","page":"391"}},{"id":"u0f7dWtv0vzteN9R","n":"Ambidexterous","ed":"dgr","kind":"merit","cat":"Physical","tag":"Merit (•)","dots":[1],"sum":"You have a high degree of off-hand dexterity, and you can perform tasks with the “wrong” hand at no penalty.","html":"<p><em>Merit (•)</em></p>\n<p>You have a high degree of off-hand dexterity, and you can perform tasks with the “wrong” hand at no penalty. You must still use the splitting rules for taking multiple actions, but you do not suffer a difficulty penalty if, say, you use two weapons or are forced to use your off hand.</p>","src":{"book":"Dark Gifts: Disciplines Restored","page":"394"}},{"id":"ujXEhotbvpwDbjpF","n":"In Both Worlds (Spiritus)","ed":"dgr","kind":"flaw","cat":"Ingrained Discipline","tag":"Flaw (•)","dots":[1],"sum":"Users of Spiritus often find themselves drifting in thoughts as their spirit is more unbound than that of most creatures inhabiting the material world.","html":"<p><em>Flaw (•)</em></p>\n<p>Users of Spiritus often find themselves drifting in thoughts as their spirit is more unbound than that of most creatures inhabiting the material world. The user suffers a three-dice penalty on all rolls to perceive something that is in the material plane.</p>","src":{"book":"Dark Gifts: Disciplines Restored","page":"390"}},{"id":"vgVTkXS3rxs75gI6","n":"Fractured Mind (Dementation)","ed":"dgr","kind":"flaw","cat":"Ingrained Discipline","tag":"Flaw (•)","dots":[1],"sum":"The vampire’s mind and sense of morality are fractured into many pieces as the picture expands. After all, is morality not a construct to enforce social insanity as sanity?","html":"<p><em>Flaw (•)</em></p>\n<p>The vampire’s mind and sense of morality are fractured into many pieces as the picture expands. After all, is morality not a construct to enforce social insanity as sanity? If the vampire adheres to Conscience, they require 2 Touchstones per Ethic to maintain it. If they adhere to a Road with the Virtue of Conviction, they may not have more than a single Ethic.</p>","src":{"book":"Dark Gifts: Disciplines Restored","page":"388"}},{"id":"vjB6yIVAw4n6uk1b","n":"Nightmares","ed":"dgr","kind":"flaw","cat":"Mental","tag":"Flaw (•)","dots":[1],"sum":"You have extraordinarily vivid “nightmares” during your sleep, which hinder your mental recovery. Characters with this Flaw recover Superficial Willpower damage at the start of session equal to the lower of their Resolve or Composure.","html":"<p><em>Flaw (•)</em></p>\n<p>You have extraordinarily vivid “nightmares” during your sleep, which hinder your mental recovery. Characters with this Flaw recover Superficial Willpower damage at the start of session equal to the lower of their Resolve or Composure. If the Attributes are equal, recover that amount -1 (example: Hiram’s Resolve and Composure are both 4, and so he only recovers 3 Superficial Willpower damage).</p>","src":{"book":"Dark Gifts: Disciplines Restored","page":"392"}},{"id":"wL9N8DwyyQAsbYCa","n":"Hale","ed":"dgr","kind":"merit","cat":"Physical","tag":"Merit (•)","dots":[1],"sum":"You are in good health and rarely if ever take ill. Gain a two-dice to resist disease whether natural or supernatural in nature","html":"<p><em>Merit (•)</em></p>\n<p>You are in good health and rarely if ever take ill. Gain a two-dice to resist disease whether natural or supernatural in nature</p>","src":{"book":"Dark Gifts: Disciplines Restored","page":"394"}},{"id":"wfj4QcDS9zo5vgkG","n":"Unbound Diabolist (Non-ghost, nondemon only)","ed":"dgr","kind":"merit","cat":"Infernal","tag":"Merit (••–••••)","dots":[2,3,4],"sum":"You once made a pact with a demon and received investments of power, but your infernal master no longer controls your life. For two points, it may be banished back to Hell or simply far away and no longer concerned with you.","html":"<p><em>Merit (••–••••)</em></p>\n<p>You once made a pact with a demon and received investments of power, but your infernal master no longer controls your life. For two points, it may be banished back to Hell or simply far away and no longer concerned with you. Either way, you can live your life with a measure of autonomy, though it is always possible your master will return one day to reclaim you.</p>\n<p>For four dots, your master has suffered final destruction at the hands of another demon (or some other powerful being) or released you from the bonds of thralldom (due to the pact struck with it or maybe it simply wanted a new thrall and got bored), leaving you truly free. Autonomy has its price, of course. Without a master, you have no way of gaining or losing Arcana and Taints. You can still improve Divinity and your Torment may rise or fall, but you can’t do anything with that potential. Thralls with the four-point version of this Merit can swear fealty to a new demon if they so desire, trading away their freedom for more power. Those with the two point version are not so lucky, since their absent masters still hold claims to their souls.</p>\n<p>Selected during character creation, gains the first dot of Divinity for free and you may set the Torment rating at whatever. You may select Taints and buy higher Divinity ratings or Arcana with experience points.</p>","src":{"book":"Dark Gifts: Disciplines Restored","page":"387"}},{"id":"xVALW1mvQOOdDo5a","n":"Inoffensive to Animals (Vampire)","ed":"dgr","kind":"merit","cat":"Supernatural","tag":"Merit (•–••)","dots":[1,2],"sum":"Unlike most Cainites, you do not cause hostile or even unsettled reactions in animals you encounter. Instead, the animals simply treat you like an ordinary human.","html":"<p><em>Merit (•–••)</em></p>\n<p></p>\n<p>Unlike most Cainites, you do not cause hostile or even unsettled reactions in animals you encounter. Instead, the animals simply treat you like an ordinary human. For one point, you can avoid upsetting a particular species and gain a die bonus to all Animal Ken rolls with them (including Animalism). For two points, you can be inoffensive to and gain a die bonus for all ordinary creatures (though those that are renowned for their affinity to the supernatural, such as black cats or owls, might still react unusually to your presence, at the Storyteller’s discretion).</p>","src":{"book":"Dark Gifts: Disciplines Restored","page":"395"}},{"id":"xgNrZdSC8YCEGPls","n":"Infernal Rituals","ed":"dgr","kind":"background","cat":"Backgrounds","tag":"","dots":[],"sum":"While raiding a hole in which one of the suspected Baali was hiding in, we fell into a trap.","html":"<p>While raiding a hole in which one of the suspected Baali was hiding in, we fell into a trap. The bastard left the writings on the wall and our ghoul men-at-arms, as mortals are always curious and dumb, paused to look at them, silently reading in this minds. Suddenly, the earth under our feet cracked wide-open and some “thing” arose from the circles and sigils drawn under us in blood.</p>\n<p>Infernal Rites are a Background that represents the possession of knowledge of rituals to bind or summon demons as well as the knowing of their Celestial Names. Infernal Rites can only be bought during character creation. Learning them after character creation requires a special project. For mechanics of using or learning Infernal Ritual, see the systems on our homebrew server if the Demons and Thralls book is done finished or have your Storyteller simply weave it as they’re quite self-explanatory by name. You may have a maximum of 6 dots in this Background at character creation. Retainers may not possess this Background, but may use them.</p>\n<p>These rituals may only be used by mortals (ghouls, revenants, changelings sorcerers and mages fall within that category). Vampires, demons and other such creatures must instead rely on their own powers to summon or bind demons. They may still purchase this Background and craft rituals as normal, but must resort to hiring or tricking mortals into performing the actual ritual. Those among the Baali who refuse to dabble in black magic frequently abuse mortal servants at their disposal to get them to perform Infernal Rites, with the easiest precaution being the blood bond to ensure that a mortal does not turn the demon on its master.</p>\n<p>When selecting this Background, you learn either a summoning or a binding ritual that is specifically crafted to work on a single demon. In addition to it, you learn the demon’s Celestial Name as it is the minimum requirement for these rituals to even work. A non-idiot summoning a demon usually also prepares a binding ritual for it unless they simply seek information or to strike a bargain with the demon to become their thrall.</p>\n<p>Demons that become available to summon or bind through the rituals learned by purchasing this Background at character creation must be discussed with the Storyteller who then builds them. While it may seem great to go for the strongest demon, know that they are more likely to tear your head off than try to reason with you should you attempt to enslave them.</p>\n<p>•• One Ritual</p>\n<p>•••• Two Rituals</p>\n<p>•••••• Three Rituals All those purchasing this Background beware: you will be destroyed for dabbling in infernalism if uncovered or you may meet a gruesome end at the hands of a spirit you tried to compel to do once your control runs out.</p>","src":{"book":"Dark Gifts: Disciplines Restored","page":"381"}},{"id":"yWte0ruLLNxZrQyD","n":"Slowdown (Temporis)","ed":"dgr","kind":"flaw","cat":"Ingrained Discipline","tag":"Flaw (•)","dots":[1],"sum":"While the users of Temporis toy with the laws of time, it creates many paradoxes that ripple through the fabric of reality which pushes back against the violator.","html":"<p><em>Flaw (•)</em></p>\n<p>While the users of Temporis toy with the laws of time, it creates many paradoxes that ripple through the fabric of reality which pushes back against the violator. Whenever the vampire fails a Rouse Check when activating a Temporis power, they sustain a point of Aggravated Health damage, and their body withers and ages before their very eyes.</p>","src":{"book":"Dark Gifts: Disciplines Restored","page":"390"}},{"id":"ywywZbDiqkya88qA","n":"Baneful Residue (Ghoul)","ed":"dgr","kind":"flaw","cat":"Ghoul and Revenant","tag":"Flaw (•)","dots":[1],"sum":"While ghouls acquire Ghoul Bane according to the Clan of their domitor, sometimes these curses can stick around long after vitae is gone. The first Ghoul Bane ghoul had is permanent.","html":"<p><em>Flaw (•)</em></p>\n<p>While ghouls acquire Ghoul Bane according to the Clan of their domitor, sometimes these curses can stick around long after vitae is gone. The first Ghoul Bane ghoul had is permanent. If they ever acquire or have acquired a new domitor, they will possess two Ghoul Banes.</p>","src":{"book":"Dark Gifts: Disciplines Restored","page":"387"}},{"id":"zZK6nN3ZW75gFlLC","n":"Scar Tissue (Fortitude)","ed":"dgr","kind":"flaw","cat":"Ingrained Discipline","tag":"Flaw (•)","dots":[1],"sum":"This Flaw impairs the vampire’s ability to maintain the appearance of health following an injury. While rousing the Blood restores Health as normal, the physical appearance of any injuries sustained remains until they rest for the day.","html":"<p><em>Flaw (•)</em></p>\n<p>This Flaw impairs the vampire’s ability to maintain the appearance of health following an injury. While rousing the Blood restores Health as normal, the physical appearance of any injuries sustained remains until they rest for the day. This can easily result in Masquerade breaches if not carefully managed.</p>","src":{"book":"Dark Gifts: Disciplines Restored","page":"389"}},{"id":"zk1ZR5L9NmDm3TQh","n":"Viccissitude Alterations","ed":"dgr","kind":"merit","cat":"Ghoul and Revenant","tag":"Merit (•–•••••)","dots":[1,2,3,4,5],"sum":"Having gone “under the knife” of a Tzimisce or some other practitioner of Vicissitude, you’ve been “gifted” certain bodily alterations.","html":"<p><em>Merit (•–•••••)</em></p>\n<p>Having gone “under the knife” of a Tzimisce or some other practitioner of Vicissitude, you’ve been “gifted” certain bodily alterations. Each dot of this Merit represents one dot of the Vicissitude rating of the vampire who performed Vicissitude on you and corresponds to the number of Attribute dots you may pay for your modifications. Even vampires may start off with this Merit, and other creatures at Storyteller’s discretion.</p>\n<p>Make sure you note the Generation of the vampire to determine whether or not you may heal it as a vampire. If your domitor is a character with Vicissitude, you do not have to buy this Merit to represent their modifications as they’ve already have their own Vicissitude rating.</p>","src":{"book":"Dark Gifts: Disciplines Restored","page":"387"}}];
